* Piezo harvester, Duffing: a frequency sweep up and down, and the jump between branches.
*
* Two beams of models/multiphysics/piezo/piezo_parts.va on the same 63.66 kOhm load, driven by
* one 2 m/s^2 sine swept 48 -> 56 -> 48 Hz over 40 s each way (`chirp`, 0.2 Hz/s): X1 linear,
* X2 with a hardening stiffness k3 = 2e8 N/m^3.
*
* Closed form (harmonic balance of the steady state, the load's damping included; computed by
* docs/examples/multiphysics/piezo_plots.py): the hardening beam's response folds over, with
* two stable branches between 51.762 and 52.809 Hz and an upper-branch peak of 0.8572 mm at
* 52.776 Hz. The linear beam peaks at 0.8937 mm at 50 Hz.
* What to look for (measured at 2.16.1+3):
*   X2 sweeping up rides the upper branch to 0.859 mm near 52.86 Hz and drops near 53.1 Hz;
*   sweeping down it stays on the lower branch until ~51.56 Hz and jumps up. The jumps sit
*   0.3 and 0.2 Hz past the folds because the sweep is not infinitely slow; at 0.1 Hz/s they
*   sit 0.19 and 0.14 Hz past (the delay past a saddle-node shrinks as the rate^(2/3)).
* Plot: python docs/examples/multiphysics/piezo_plots.py target/release/va-cli
* Run: va-cli sim circuits/multiphysics/piezo_duffing.net --model models/multiphysics/piezo --tran --report X1.x,X2.x
XS acc chirp amp=2 f0=48 f1=56 tsw=40
X1 acc p1 gnd piezo_beam
R1 p1 gnd 63662
X2 acc p2 gnd piezo_beam k3=2e8
R2 p2 gnd 63662
.tran 0.1m 80
.end
